From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: musb: gadget: Do not flush a waiting RX packet on
 restart
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Without this, a bulk OUT packet that arrives before the function driver
has queued its next request is lost, because musb_ep_restart() flushes
the RX FIFO when the request is queued. The host has already got its ACK
and does not send the packet again. With the mass storage gadget the
CBW of the next command disappears, the data phase is never started
and the host runs into a command timeout.

musb_g_rx() finds no request and leaves the packet, with RxPktRdy set,
in the FIFO. The flush that commit 3fc137386c46 ("usb: musb: Fix
hardware lockup on first Rx endpoint request") added in place of
rxstate() then throws it away. That commit did so because rxstate()
could call ->complete() from within usb_ep_queue(), which is not
allowed. A callback such as rx_complete() of u_ether enables IRQs
through netif_rx() when it runs in task context, so the interrupt of a
second waiting packet found an empty queue and RxPktRdy stayed set
with no interrupt left to unload it.

Do not flush. If RxPktRdy is set when the request is started, queue a
bottom half work item, which takes the lock and calls musb_g_rx() as
the interrupt would have. The packet is unloaded into the request, so
RxPktRdy does not stay set. The lockup cannot come back: the callback
no longer runs inside usb_ep_queue() but in softirq context with the
IRQs off, where in_interrupt() is true as in the interrupt path, so
netif_rx() does not disable and enable the bottom halves and no IRQ is
enabled during the callback. If the callback queues the next request
while another packet waits, the restart queues the work again. Without
RxPktRdy the FIFO holds nothing to flush, and the restart leaves the
endpoint alone. The restart holds a runtime PM reference until the work
has run, as the work cannot resume the controller in softirq context.

Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S, where USB0 runs as a mass storage gadget:
the host timed out on a command in 5 of 7 runs.

Not covered: a second packet in a double-buffered FIFO that is already
waiting without a pending interrupt while the request is not complete
after the first packet.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: with USB0 as a mass storage gadget, alone and
together with ACM and NCM, the host read the whole LUN without a command
timeout in every run.

Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Cc: Hubert Wiśniewski <hubert.wisniewski.25632@gmail.com>
Fixes: 3fc137386c46 ("usb: musb: Fix hardware lockup on first Rx endpoint request")
Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/usb/musb/musb_core.h   |  2 ++
 drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c | 50 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 49 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_core.h b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_core.h
index 91b5b6b66f96..eb0bbad9a4d5 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_core.h
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_core.h
@@ -288,6 +288,8 @@ struct musb {
 	struct delayed_work	deassert_reset_work;
 	struct delayed_work	finish_resume_work;
 	struct delayed_work	gadget_work;
+	struct work_struct	gadget_rx_work;
+	u16			gadget_rx_eps;	/* RX endpoints with a waiting packet */
 	u16			hwvers;
 
 	u16			intrrxe;
diff --git a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
index 016d3f3fc1e0..8e4b1fd6bea4 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_gadget.c
@@ -1170,11 +1170,52 @@ void musb_ep_restart(struct musb *musb, struct musb_request *req)
 	if (req->tx) {
 		txstate(musb, req);
 	} else {
+		/*
+		 * A packet that arrived before this request was queued is
+		 * still in the FIFO: musb_g_rx() found no request and left
+		 * it. The host has acknowledged it and will not send it
+		 * again, so do not flush it. Unloading it here could call
+		 * ->complete() from within usb_ep_queue(), so hand it to a
+		 * bottom half work, which runs musb_g_rx() as the interrupt
+		 * would have. The controller is active here; keep it so
+		 * until the work has run.
+		 */
 		csr = musb_readw(epio, MUSB_RXCSR);
-		csr |= MUSB_RXCSR_FLUSHFIFO | MUSB_RXCSR_P_WZC_BITS;
-		musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
-		musb_writew(epio, MUSB_RXCSR, csr);
+		if (csr & MUSB_RXCSR_RXPKTRDY) {
+			musb->gadget_rx_eps |= BIT(req->epnum);
+			pm_runtime_get_noresume(musb->controller);
+			if (!queue_work(system_bh_wq, &musb->gadget_rx_work))
+				pm_runtime_put_noidle(musb->controller);
+		}
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Unload the packets that were waiting when their request was queued.
+ * Runs in softirq context, so a ->complete() callback sees the same context
+ * as from the interrupt and netif_rx() does not toggle the bottom halves.
+ * It cannot sleep and does not resume the controller: musb_ep_restart()
+ * ran with the controller active and holds a reference until the work
+ * has run.
+ */
+static void musb_gadget_rx_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct musb *musb = container_of(work, struct musb, gadget_rx_work);
+	unsigned long flags;
+	unsigned long eps;
+	unsigned int epnum;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&musb->lock, flags);
+	eps = musb->gadget_rx_eps;
+	musb->gadget_rx_eps = 0;
+	for_each_set_bit(epnum, &eps, BITS_PER_LONG) {
+		musb_ep_select(musb->mregs, epnum);
+		if (musb_readw(musb->endpoints[epnum].regs, MUSB_RXCSR) &
+		    MUSB_RXCSR_RXPKTRDY)
+			musb_g_rx(musb, epnum);
 	}
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&musb->lock, flags);
+	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(musb->controller);
 }
 
 static int musb_ep_restart_resume_work(struct musb *musb, void *data)
@@ -1787,6 +1828,7 @@ int musb_gadget_setup(struct musb *musb)
 	/* don't support otg protocols */
 	musb->g.is_otg = 0;
 	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&musb->gadget_work, musb_gadget_work);
+	INIT_WORK(&musb->gadget_rx_work, musb_gadget_rx_work);
 	musb_g_init_endpoints(musb);
 
 	musb->is_active = 0;
@@ -1810,6 +1852,8 @@ void musb_gadget_cleanup(struct musb *musb)
 
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&musb->gadget_work);
 	usb_del_gadget_udc(&musb->g);
+	if (cancel_work_sync(&musb->gadget_rx_work))
+		pm_runtime_put_noidle(musb->controller);
 }
 
